+    <p><b>There is no single accuracy percentage we can honestly promise.</b> The text tool is an English-focused pattern model trained on public research collections, and it has not been validated as an authorship detector. Its 0–100 pattern signal is not a probability.</p>
     <p>The bundled GAIC Image Model v2 is derived from the <a href="https://huggingface.co/OwensLab/commfor-model-224" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Community Forensics 224 model</a>, trained on content from thousands of generators available through 2024. It can still miss newer generators, heavily compressed or edited media, screenshots, small images, and unfamiliar content. GAIC therefore uses only a conservative high-signal warning band and never labels a low score “authentic.”</p>
     <p>These limits are not unique to GAIC. OpenAI <a href="https://openai.com/index/new-ai-classifier-for-indicating-ai-written-text/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">withdrew its own text classifier</a> for low accuracy and warned against primary-decision use. Research has also documented <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11156" target="_blank" rel="noopener">detector evasion and fundamental limits</a> and <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02819" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bias against non-native English writing</a>. That is why GAIC asks you to check drafts, source history, and context instead of accusing someone from a score.</p>
 
